export const FREE_CHAPTER_V3_TITLE = "The Voice That Keeps You Stuck";
export const FREE_CHAPTER_V3_HTML: string = "<p>It is 3:10 a.m. and Andre is awake again.</p>\n<p>He wasn\u2019t thinking about anything. Then a sentence surfaced from\nSunday dinner at his in-laws\u2019. His brother-in-law, Gil, had raised a\nglass and said, \u201cTo Andre\u2019s new business plan. Step one: hope.\u201d Everyone\nlaughed. Andre laughed too. Then he passed the potatoes.</p>\n<p>Now he is back at that table. In this version he puts his fork down\nand says, calmly, \u201cI\u2019ve had six paying clients since March, Gil. How\u2019s\nyour side project going?\u201d The table goes quiet. His wife squeezes his\nhand.</p>\n<p>He runs it again with a sharper line. Then a kinder one. Then one\nthat would end the matter forever. By four o\u2019clock he knows every\nversion except the one he could actually say.</p>\n<p>He is fifty-two. He runs a small kitchen-and-bath remodeling company\nthat is finally starting to work. He handles difficult customers every\nweek without trouble. So why did he go blank at a dinner table?</p>\n<p>And why, when he thinks about mentioning it to Gil, does a reasonable\nvoice say, <em>It was just a joke. Don\u2019t be so sensitive. You\u2019ll ruin\nthe next family dinner</em>?</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>In this chapter:</strong><br />\n- Tell when the inner voice is protecting you and when it is keeping you\nstuck.<br />\n- Replace endless preparing with one small, real action.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"the-replay-is-a-signal\">The replay is a signal</h2>\n<p>Most advice treats the 3 a.m. replay as a problem to quiet down. I\nthink that misreads what\u2019s happening.</p>\n<p>The replay is often your mind doing a review you didn\u2019t get to do in\nthe moment. Maybe a line was crossed and you paid for it. Maybe you\u2019re\njust worried about how you came across. The replay alone can\u2019t tell you\nwhich, so it\u2019s worth looking at what was actually said and done before\ndeciding what it means.</p>\n<p>The trouble is that the replay rarely finishes the job. It hands you\na perfect line twelve hours late, then plays the scene again. Research\non rumination, going over the same bad moment again and again, finds\nthat it tends to deepen a low mood and get in the way of solving the\nproblem. What you need is something you can use in the present, or a\ncalm way to go back later.</p>\n<p>Going blank is not a failure. When you\u2019re surprised, embarrassed or\nunder pressure, your body tends to put safety first and clever replies a\ndistant second. Most people find their words afterward, and that doesn\u2019t\nmean you missed your only chance. Many moments can be revisited a day or\na week later, and a calm sentence said on Wednesday often works better\nthan a sharp one said on Sunday.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-defense-attorney-you-didnt-hire\">The defense attorney you\ndidn\u2019t hire</h2>\n<p>Picture a courtroom. You have a fair complaint. You were mocked,\ninterrupted, overlooked or taken for granted. Now picture who stands up\nto argue the case.</p>\n<p>For many reliable people, the most persuasive lawyer in the room\nworks for the other side. And that lawyer is in their own head.</p>\n<p><em>He\u2019s under a lot of stress.</em> <em>It was probably a\nmisunderstanding.</em> <em>You\u2019ve done the same thing yourself.</em>\n<em>Bringing it up will only make things worse.</em></p>\n<p>Any of those might be partly true. The problem is that the voice only\nargues in one direction. It defends the person who hurt you, and it\nprosecutes you for wanting to say so. It is an over-eager defense\nattorney the other side got for free.</p>\n<p>That matters, because much of the work of pushing you around is done\nbefore anyone else lifts a finger. A thoughtless boss doesn\u2019t have to\nrefuse your raise if you never ask. Gil doesn\u2019t have to defend his joke\nif Andre talks himself out of mentioning it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"where-the-voice-comes-from\">Where the voice comes from</h2>\n<p>Voices like this come from different places for different people. For\nsome, it echoes things heard many times growing up: \u201cDon\u2019t be so\ndramatic.\u201d \u201cWho do you think you are?\u201d For others, it grew out of a hard\njob, a bad breakup, a recent setback, or pressure they\u2019re facing right\nnow. Sometimes predicting criticism once helped someone avoid it, and\nthe habit stayed after things changed.</p>\n<p>You don\u2019t need to trace it to a cause. One useful question when it\nspeaks is: <em>does that sound like someone I know?</em> If it does,\nhearing the line as theirs can take away some of its authority. If it\ndoesn\u2019t, that\u2019s fine too. You can still check what it says.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-the-voice-says\">What the voice says</h2>\n<p>The voice has a small vocabulary. Most of its lines do one of five\njobs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>It shrinks the problem.</strong> \u201cIt\u2019s not a big deal.\u201d \u201cIt\nwas just a joke.\u201d</li>\n<li><strong>It explains away their behavior.</strong> \u201cHe\u2019s stressed.\u201d\n\u201cThat\u2019s just how she is.\u201d</li>\n<li><strong>It turns it on you.</strong> \u201cYou\u2019re too sensitive.\u201d \u201cMaybe\nyou misremembered.\u201d</li>\n<li><strong>It predicts disaster.</strong> \u201cThey\u2019ll be furious.\u201d \u201cYou\u2019ll\nruin the holiday.\u201d</li>\n<li><strong>It questions your right to ask.</strong> \u201cWho are you to\nwant more?\u201d \u201cBe grateful.\u201d</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Several of these are exactly what a pushy person would say out loud\nif you raised the issue. The voice and the person in front of you often\nuse the same script. That\u2019s one reason the script works so well.</p>\n<h2 id=\"when-the-voice-is-right\">When the voice is right</h2>\n<p>To be fair, the voice is sometimes right. Sometimes you are tired and\nmisread a message. Sometimes waiting really is wiser. Sometimes a\nsituation reminds you of something old and painful, and your reaction is\nbigger than the moment.</p>\n<p>The aim isn\u2019t to ignore it. The aim is to stop treating it as the\njudge. Check its predictions against what actually happens. The voice\nsaid your manager would be annoyed if you asked about the training\nbudget. Was she? Try it with a small request to someone you feel safe\nwith. Write the prediction down, then write the result, somewhere\nprivate. Over a few weeks you will see which way it tends to lean. In\nseven studies, people who said no to a request expected worse fallout\nthan they actually got. Many people find the voice overestimates the\ndanger of speaking up and underestimates the cost of staying quiet.</p>\n<h2 id=\"always-almost-ready\">Always almost ready</h2>\n<p>The voice has a quieter trick, and it can cost more than any single\nconversation.</p>\n<p>For two years, Natalie has wanted to apply for the team lead role in\nher department. First she wanted to finish a leadership course. Then she\nwanted to wait until after the reorganization. Then she drafted an email\nto her manager asking what the role required. It has been sitting in her\ndrafts folder for five months. She rereads it every few weeks and\nchanges a word or two.</p>\n<p>This is <strong>always almost ready</strong>: preparing endlessly,\nwaiting to feel confident, taking the course before the ask, polishing\nthe draft that never gets sent.</p>\n<p>It feels responsible. It looks like diligence. And it does protect\nyou. As long as you are still getting ready, nobody can say no. You\ncan\u2019t be turned down for something you never asked for.</p>\n<p>The cost is slower and larger. Someone else applies. The years pass.\nNatalie is still getting ready, while the role goes to someone who\nsimply asked.</p>\n<p>Some preparation helps. But if every finished task leads to another\nreason to wait, readiness has become the excuse. It is usually a feeling\nthat comes after action, not before it. Natalie doesn\u2019t have to feel\nready to lead the team before asking what the role requires.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-give-the-voice-a-different-job\">How to give the voice a\ndifferent job</h2>\n<p>You don\u2019t have to silence the voice. Arguing with it in your head\nusually turns into another replay. It\u2019s trying to protect you. You can\nkeep that intention and give it better instructions.</p>\n<p><strong>1. Name it.</strong> Give the voice a name, even a slightly\nsilly one: \u201cthe Lawyer,\u201d or the name of the person it sounds like. When\nit starts up, think, \u201cThat\u2019s the Lawyer again.\u201d Andre calls his \u201cUncle\nLou,\u201d after the relative who told him to stop bragging.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Finish the trial.</strong> When the voice makes the case\nfor the other person, make the case for yourself with the same care.\n\u201cGil was joking\u201d can stay. Now add: \u201cAnd he said it in front of eleven\npeople, and he\u2019s made the same joke twice before, and it bothered me.\u201d\nYou don\u2019t need a verdict. You just stop letting one side speak\nunopposed.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Use the friend test.</strong> If a good friend told you\nthis exact story, what would you say? Most people are far fairer to a\nfriend than to themselves.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Take the smallest real step.</strong> Not the full speech,\nand not another round of preparation. Something that actually reaches\nanother person. Natalie sends the email asking what the team lead role\nrequires. Andre decides he will mention the joke to Gil, in one or two\nsentences, when they\u2019re alone.</p>\n<p>If the voice wins for a while and you don\u2019t send the email, that\u2019s\nnormal. Make the step smaller: one question about the role instead of\nthe whole case for promoting you. And if the person brushes you off, or\ntells others you can\u2019t take a joke, one brush-off isn\u2019t a pattern. If\nyour concerns keep getting turned back on you, Chapter 6, A Bad Moment\nor a Pattern?, helps you look at it clearly.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Try saying:</strong><br />\n\u201cSomething you said on Sunday stuck with me. Do you have a minute?\u201d\n(warm)<br />\n\u201cI didn\u2019t love the joke about my business. I\u2019d rather you didn\u2019t make it\nagain.\u201d (direct)<br />\n\u201cPlease don\u2019t joke about my business in front of the family again.\u201d\n(final)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"andre-calls-gil\">Andre calls Gil</h2>\n<p>Andre calls Gil on Wednesday evening. He has decided to say it once\nand keep it short.</p>\n<p><strong>Andre:</strong> \u201cHey, Gil. Got a minute? Something from\nSunday has been on my mind.\u201d</p>\n<p><strong>Gil:</strong> \u201cSure. What\u2019s up?\u201d</p>\n<p><strong>Andre:</strong> \u201cThe toast about my business plan being hope.\nI laughed along, but it didn\u2019t sit right with me.\u201d</p>\n<p><strong>Gil:</strong> \u201cOh, come on. It was a joke. You know I\u2019m\nalways kidding around.\u201d</p>\n<p><em>The voice in Andre\u2019s head agrees with Gil instantly.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Andre:</strong> \u201cI know you were kidding. I\u2019m still telling\nyou it bothered me.\u201d</p>\n<p><strong>Gil:</strong> \u201cMan, you\u2019ve gotten sensitive since you went\nout on your own.\u201d</p>\n<p><strong>Andre:</strong> \u201cMaybe. The business matters a lot to me\nright now, and it\u2019s working. I\u2019d rather it wasn\u2019t the joke at family\ndinners.\u201d</p>\n<p><strong>Gil:</strong> \u201cEverybody laughed, including you.\u201d</p>\n<p><strong>Andre:</strong> \u201cI did. I wish I\u2019d said something then. I\u2019m\nsaying it now.\u201d</p>\n<p><em>A pause.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Gil:</strong> \u201cFine. I won\u2019t bring up the business.\nHappy?\u201d</p>\n<p><strong>Andre:</strong> \u201cThanks. That\u2019s all I wanted.\u201d</p>\n<p><strong>Gil:</strong> \u201cOkay. See you at Maria\u2019s birthday, I\nguess.\u201d</p>\n<p>Gil doesn\u2019t apologize. He sounds annoyed. He may still make a crack\nnext time, and Andre may go blank again.</p>\n<p>But the replay has stopped being a speech at 3 a.m. Andre turned it\ninto two calm sentences, said to the right person, at a time he chose.\nAnd if Gil repeats the joke, Andre has already said where he stands.</p>\n<h2 id=\"and-sometimes-the-voice-is-warning-you\">And sometimes the voice\nis warning you</h2>\n<p>Sometimes the voice is warning you about something real. If raising\nan issue with someone has led to threats, rage or punishment, the voice\nmay be protecting your safety, not your comfort. Don\u2019t test a new\napproach with that person. Chapter 11 is about getting real help.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Try this today:</strong> Find one thing you have been \u201calmost\nready\u201d to do: an email, a question, an application. Do the smallest real\npart of it today. Send the question, book the call, or ask what the\nrequirements are. Then write down what the voice predicted, and later\nwhat actually happened.</p>\n</blockquote>\n";
